      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
